  <section id="best-for">
    <h2>Who Art of Animation is best for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Families of five or six.</strong> The family suites sleep up to six, with two bathrooms and a kitchenette, at a Value resort price.</li>
      <li><strong>Anyone who wants the Skyliner at a Value rate.</strong> The station is on the Generation Gap Bridge, shared with Pop Century, and it goes to EPCOT and Hollywood Studios.</li>
      <li><strong>Smaller parties watching their budget.</strong> The Little Mermaid rooms cost less than the suites. They're a standard Value room, just a longer walk from Animation Hall.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Think twice if…</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The pool or resort dining is the point of your trip.</strong> There's a food court, not a sit-down restaurant. The Big Blue Pool is huge, and it can feel crowded.</li>
      <li><strong>You want Extended Evening Hours.</strong> Value resort guests don't get them, and on those EPCOT nights the Skyliner route to Art of Animation shuts down early.</li>
      <li><strong>Your trip falls on Jan 24–30, 2027.</strong> The Skyliner is closed for refurbishment then, and buses fill in.</li>
      <li><strong>You're in a Little Mermaid room and you'd rather not walk.</strong> That section sits farthest from the lobby, food court and Skyliner.</li>
    </ul>
